592186045417] [17592186231520 #edb/ref 1045 "Resonance in Vibrating Systems" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231520 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186231830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231520 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231520 #edb/ref 1050 1500.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231520 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "1173ef06-74e3-5b10-8cad-8816bd626835" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231830 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186231831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231830 #edb/ref 1061 612647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231830 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "358b4213-7b6b-5471-a600-b592535b19ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231830 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186231985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231831 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186231832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231831 #edb/ref 1061 612648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231831 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4d89af0c-6f61-561d-ba3a-9fe8f3ec929d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231831 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186231427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231832 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186231833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231832 #edb/ref 1061 612651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231832 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5658d94f-9b6c-586a-9fb4-a29a2128db9e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231832 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186231428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231833 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186231834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231833 #edb/ref 1061 612654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231833 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "35c65152-fe66-57ca-8c82-a01849b005d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231833 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186231987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231834 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186231835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231834 #edb/ref 1061 612655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231834 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "eb6f14ff-8487-50a4-a45b-1ff02e60483d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231834 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186231429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231835 #edb/ref 1061 612658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231835 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d14184e9-93ef-5eca-99aa-b25660380309" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231835 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186231988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231985 #edb/ref 1064 "A standard vibrating system under an external force ![](images/f6/f6ec0c498eee67c8bceaf8cf937f9b77f6395bd4ad28df6da30ca42622366f99.svg) looks like\n![](images/bd/bd2f22daabf1b333259361452745a963a4fd08c93cf70178eb0fb7a64218fdd1.svg)\nwhere ![](images/2c/2cb3a34686b80c6ea9a5a2e433d1f804b765e1174532b15435f471746ffbd3af.svg) is the system's natural **internal frequency**.\n\nThe complementary solution ![](images/02/024420a1f4a1300a242618c4742c03a91e27ee710937bb23acd902722711833c.svg) describes the system's internal oscillation when there is no external force. It can be written as\n![](images/95/95b99f0f2a3303b1ae2b3cafa4d0df18925f01ed684f71311bda48ace70c1b4b.svg)\nso the motion naturally oscillates at frequency ![](images/70/70a39e6c2338362b95e1ca575f5881d5c55759902897a19735596bc717c7afcc.svg)\n\nIf the external force ![](images/f6/f6ec0c498eee67c8bceaf8cf937f9b77f6395bd4ad28df6da30ca42622366f99.svg) is periodic, then it has its own forcing frequency ![](images/9d/9d61bc8820c203579d5753afc12cf659e4aaef1e7e7d0ab448c34719972a3e73.svg) **Resonance** occurs when the forcing frequency matches the natural frequency:\n![](images/24/24b644830cfc3be4d93f3a97f082c3257d5fb20c016daa74db4ebf21eef732e9.svg)\n\nIntuitively, resonance happens because the force repeatedly \"pushes\" the system at exactly the right rhythm each cycle. In the undamped model, these pushes continually add energy to the system at just the right phase, causing the oscillation amplitude to grow without bound.\n\nFor example, suppose the position ![](images/a1/a11c6d83010d49a22f82075f22ecafa791d24bef37e65fa46d4bce6a795ac213.svg) measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n![](images/70/7076ff3f6735079d869815f4e815b7904a1a083fbe5a2e5d2281adfa270c375e.svg)\nwhere ![](images/dd/dde902d242b218395adbef9bfc212e62665b9dd9f239fc735fab8eb7325d2175.svg) is the time, in seconds. Let's find the value of the parameter ![](images/48/48c7b594e8628a17b991d4e65e7bee6ffe6680dd410f036cfae83154bbd55fc6.svg) that causes the oscillator to resonate.\n\nNotice that the complementary solution has frequency ![](images/12/123dfb2143795c6c18189c8c267c39923598d8998875b966ce5a58a50796b170.svg) where ![](images/63/63b6e1d88c2e6c5a4db5966b5c6711403b91091065c54ae909620e0aa2a00111.svg) Therefore,\n![](images/04/0435ca12e088811ffad2b9fcc7d54a58b69f590bcb85657807e16eced9dd6470.svg)\n\nThe external force is\n![](images/19/191e5f6b5169e36e08215529683d79bad0046a39058c73741d4102c095bdf3bd.svg)\nwhich has forcing frequency ![](images/bf/bf89fc164205f4db41f1307668c4f114944b3478bd4c6c5f04dcda6452b67c7f.svg)\n\nResonance occurs when ![](images/bc/bc1f9bcb484213078132916bc6c9a42689f95ea470453d810294900e25c8597e.svg) so\n![](images/4f/4fec299df0cb32cc9077f3a3438885e1aa1d2f9db239d82c4f5b8a437ec04f93.svg)\n\nLet's see more examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231985 #edb/ref 1065 14762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231985 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b61c68de-4314-5d91-bcb2-75fd1ed98232" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231985 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231987 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider an undamped forced oscillator,\n\n$$\ny''+\\omega^2y=f(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\omega$ is the system's natural frequency. Its general solution is\n\n$$\ny=y_c+y_p,\n$$\n\nwhere $y_c$ solves the homogeneous equation $y''+\\omega^2y=0$ and $y_p$ is any particular solution to the full equation.\n\nNotice that the complementary solution can be written in any of the following equivalent forms:\n\n1\\. $y_c = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n2\\. $y_c = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n3\\. $y_c =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}$\n\nNow, suppose the external force has the resonant form\n\n$$\nf(t)=a\\cos(\\omega t)+b\\sin(\\omega t),\n$$\n\nso that the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega.$\n\nA sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega$ would duplicate $y_c,$ so at resonance we multiply by $t$ and use a trial of the form\n\n$$\ny_p=t\\bigl(C\\cos(\\omega t)+D\\sin(\\omega t)\\bigr).\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$ and $D,$ we first compute derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)+t(-C\\omega \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)+D\\omega \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & =2(-C\\omega \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)+D\\omega \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t))-t{\\omega }^{2}(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the equation, we have that the $t$-terms cancel out, and we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}{y}_{p}=2\\omega D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)-2\\omega C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we need $y_p''+\\omega^2y_p=f(t)=a\\cos(\\omega t)+b\\sin(\\omega t),$ equating coefficients gives\n\n$$\n2\\omega D=a,\n\\qquad\n-2\\omega C=b.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nC=-\\frac{b}{2\\omega},\n\\qquad\nD=\\frac{a}{2\\omega},\n$$\n\nso a standard resonant particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p =\\frac{t}{2\\omega}\\bigl(-b\\cos(\\omega t)+a\\sin(\\omega t)\\bigr).\n$$\n\nSo, when $f(t)=a\\cos(\\omega t)+b\\sin(\\omega t)$ and resonance occurs, the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{y=A\\cos(\\omega t)+B\\sin(\\omega t)+\\dfrac{t}{2\\omega}\\bigl(-b\\cos(\\omega t)+a\\sin(\\omega t)\\bigr)}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231987 #edb/ref 1065 14777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231987 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b19a33b7-fd81-538c-8139-d44653c47533" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231987 #edb/ref 1067 "General Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations With Resonance" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231988 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that resonance is the phenomenon where an external periodic force drives a vibrating system at exactly the system's natural frequency. In the undamped model, this causes the oscillations to grow larger and larger over time.\n\nIn the ideal undamped model, resonance produces a particular solution that grows in magnitude with time (due to the extra factor of $t$). This is why the model predicts that the amplitude increases *without bound*.\n\nFor example, consider the undamped forced oscillator\n\n$$\ny'' + 9y = \\sin(3t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution is of the form $y_c = A\\cos(3t) + B\\sin(3t),$ the natural frequency of the system is $\\omega = 3,$ and the forcing frequency is also $3.$ Because these frequencies match, resonance occurs.\n\nSince the forcing term $\\sin(3t)$ duplicates a term in the homogeneous solution, we must multiply our guess by $t.$ We try a solution of the form $y_p = t(C\\cos(3t) + D\\sin(3t)).$\n\nCalculating the derivatives of $y_p,$ substituting them into the equation, and equating coefficients of $\\sin(3t)$ and $\\cos(3t)$ yields $C=-\\dfrac16$ and $D=0.$ So, the particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\ny_p = -\\dfrac{t}{6}\\cos(3t).\n$$\n\nNotice that this solution grows larger as $t$ increases. This time-dependent growth illustrates how resonance leads to an increase in oscillation amplitude in the undamped model.\n\nIn real systems, perfectly *unbounded growth* does not occur because physical effects, such as damping and energy loss, eventually limit the amplitude. Even so, forcing near the natural frequency can still produce very large oscillations, which is why resonance is important in engineering design." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231988 #edb/ref 1065 14778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231988 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4f439c3c-eac7-5d41-a8b1-fcbe2eb8d44b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231988 #edb/ref 1067 "Practical Resonance" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315218 #edb/ref 1080 "10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315218 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315218 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "105f4c4e-d4f2-5528-bfdd-59865716d537" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315559 #edb/ref 1080 "5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315559 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315559 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4d4de519-7dc3-5a63-91dc-88fb097de927" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316079 #edb/ref 1080 "8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316079 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316079 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "aaaa23f1-3d6f-5700-8adf-0ab757d01cc9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316277 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{18}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316277 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316277 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cca9f77e-1eac-5dd3-b2b8-a6a15bf83357" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316745 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186315218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316745 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316745 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316745 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186315218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316745 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "5551b954-fda2-5f2d-abc7-527dd2646792" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316816 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186316277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316816 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316816 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316816 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316816 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186316277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316816 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6fa88ca7-4486-52c4-a8ca-dfdd856acf42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316851 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186315559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316851 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316851 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316851 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186315559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316851 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "80e5b3fd-f8d6-55a8-9a84-9bc4da788fc1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316864 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186316079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316864 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316864 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316864 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186316079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316864 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8b00e565-5dc1-5b1f-8a94-9d5cc2730e81" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317423 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317423 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343699" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317423 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317423 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle of mass $0.5\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force \n\n$$\nF(t)=2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{N}\n$$\n\nis applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317423 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c9d9c170-a8c4-5910-b604-3da53c4eb5a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317423 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.5{y}^{″}=-ky+2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2ky=4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=2k\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$ Since\n\n$$\nf(t)=4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)=2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)),\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $4.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {4}^{2}=8\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317433 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186468361 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186317433 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186468362 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186317433 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186468363 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186317433 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343764" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317433 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317433 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186317433 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7120fcf0-d3de-513a-93a7-21786701d7c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317433 #edb/ref 1074 "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $3.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =3.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =3$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[(6D-9Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-(6C+9Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)]+9t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ 6D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-6C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=0$ and $D=\\frac{5}{3}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nSolutions I, II, and III match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333450 #edb/ref 1080 "p=\\sqrt{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333450 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333450 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3210051c-3999-5418-903e-b15e38e02322" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333513 #edb/ref 1080 "p=9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333513 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333513 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "38b5b020-4f8f-567f-a7cc-df5b75d4aa8d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333903 #edb/ref 1080 "p=\\frac{9}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333903 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333903 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "64c4f9a2-40a9-51cb-89ef-5dd5f4612358" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334306 #edb/ref 1080 "p=3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334306 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334306 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "93fa9c39-59bb-537d-b57b-efe61e7a1d62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334623 #edb/ref 1080 "p=\\frac{3}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334623 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334623 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bb46ab48-8ea8-59c5-8936-9b90e3e9cf81" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335574 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335574 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333513 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335574 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335574 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335574 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335574 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335574 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335574 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186334306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335574 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b47f96a3-e6a8-5ed6-8d03-4b9a5051d156" #edb/ref 17592186